# Commits `git blame` should look through, so it reaches the commit that
# actually wrote a line rather than the one that only reshaped it.
#
# Activate per clone (git does not read this file by default, and a fresh
# clone does not inherit the setting):
#
#     git config blame.ignoreRevsFile .git-blame-ignore-revs
#
# One full 40-character SHA per line. This file is EMPTY until the M047
# normalization lands: a squash merge writes a new commit on the default
# branch, so the branch-side SHA is never an ancestor of main and naming it
# would have no effect.
#
# Note when the M047 commit is added: it is not a pure whitespace commit. The
# squash carries `.gitattributes`, the check script, the CI job, and doc
# changes alongside the renormalization, so blame will look through those
# additions too. That is the accepted cost of un-attributing the 18
# renormalized files.
